WEBVTT
TITLE The Comparison Lower Bound: Decision Trees, Leaf Counting and the Exact Height — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Sort eight items and count the comparisons: merge sort spends 17, insertion sort 28 in the worst case, selection sort 28.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Sixteen sits under all three. Not because the algorithms share code, but because the problem itself forbids fewer.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The count that matters is orders, not items: eight items have 8! = 40,320 possible input orders.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Each comparison answers one yes-or-no question, so it can at most double the number of orders an algorithm has told apart.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Draw the algorithm as a tree. Each internal node is one comparison; the two arrows out of it are the two possible answers.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Follow one path from the root to a leaf and you have run the algorithm once: those comparisons, in that order, on one input.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
For three items the whole tree has five comparison nodes and six leaves. Every leaf prints one sorted order, and no leaf is reached by two inputs.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
So the tree is a binary tree, its leaves are the orders it can distinguish, and the worst case is its height.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
A correct algorithm must be able to output any of the n! orders. So it needs at least n! leaves, one per order.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
For three items that is six leaves. The tree of height three has eight slots at the bottom, so two slots go unused.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
For four items it is 24 leaves against 16 slots at height four, so height four is already impossible before any algorithm is written.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
This is why the argument is called information theoretic: it counts outcomes, and never looks inside the algorithm.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
A binary tree of height h has at most 2 to the h leaves: one at the root, doubling at every level down.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Combine the two facts: n! leaves are needed, and 2 to the h is all a height h tree can hold. So 2 to the h is at least n!.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Take logs and round up: the height is at least the ceiling of log base two of n factorial. That is the lower bound.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
For five items: 2 to the 6 is 64, still short of 120, and 2 to the 7 is 128, so seven comparisons are necessary.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Here is the computed table. The fourth column is the answer to the exam question: the least height that can hold n! leaves.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
n = 3 gives 3, n = 4 gives 5, n = 5 gives 7, n = 8 gives 16, and n = 12 gives 29. Those five are worth memorising.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
n = 1 needs no comparison, and n = 2 needs one. The formula has to give those too, which it does.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The gap between n! and the power of two above it is the slack. It is why the answer is a ceiling and never a round number.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Rounding down is not a rounding error, it is an impossibility: for five items, six comparisons hold only 64 of the 120 orders.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
A comparison of two distinct items has three outcomes, but only two of them can happen, so the tree stays binary.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
That is why the base is two and not three. Using log base three would give about 4.36 for five items, which is far too optimistic.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
One more reason the count is a whole number: a height counts comparisons, and half a comparison does not exist.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
For large n, Stirling replaces n! by an expression: log base two of n! is about n log n minus 1.4427 n plus a half log n.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
The leading term is n log base two n. The rest is lower order, so the bound is Theta of n log n, not merely Omega.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Merge sort needs n ceiling of log n, minus the next power of two, plus one: 17 at n = 8, against a floor of 16.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
That is the whole asymptotic story: a floor of n log n, an algorithm that meets it, and a constant that never matters.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
The bound is a floor, not a promise. Up to n = 11 an algorithm reaches it exactly: 3, 5, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 26.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
At n = 12 the ceiling is 29 and the proven minimum is 30. One comparison of slack the counting cannot see.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
The gap is not monotone: it is one from 12 to 19, then zero at 20 and 21, then one again at 22.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
For the exam: use the ceiling for the bound, and only claim a minimum where the paper says it is achievable.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
The bound counts comparisons. Counting sort compares nothing: it reads each key as an index and tallies it.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
No comparisons means no decision tree, so the counting argument has nothing to count. The floor does not apply.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Radix sort makes no comparisons either: it sorts digit by digit, so its cost depends on the number of digits.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
So the floor is real and conditional: it binds every method that learns only by comparing, and nothing else.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
The same tree bounds the average: with L leaves, the average depth is at least log base two of L.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
A lopsided tree pays for its shallow leaves with very deep ones. The average is what forbids that trade.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Substituting n! gives an average floor of log base two of n!, the same n log n as the worst case.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
But the best case is not bounded: insertion sort finishes a sorted array in n minus 1 comparisons, well under the floor.

41
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.500
Step one: decide what is being counted. Comparisons in the worst case, in the average case, or on one input?

42
00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:15.000
Step two: write 2 to the h is at least n!, then find the smallest power of two that covers n factorial.

43
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.500
Step three: say whether the number is a floor or an achievable minimum. They differ from n = 12 onward.

44
00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:30.000
Step four: name the model. Comparisons only, so a non-comparison sort is outside the bound. Step five: check the base is two.

